Startup Simulator - Foundational Game Structure and Design Direction

Startup Simulator is a business-building game centered on manufacturing, growth, and time management. At its core, the game tells the story of a single entrepreneur beginning with a small garage workshop and gradually building a successful manufacturing company. Although the player interacts with machines, materials, employees, and customers, the true focus of the game is the transformation of time and effort into profit. Every action, from researching markets to operating equipment, exists to move the company toward greater capability, larger contracts, and ultimately the victory condition of one million dollars in cumulative profit.

The game begins modestly. The player starts at home with two primary spaces: a home office and a garage workshop. The home office serves as the administrative center of the company. Through the office computer, the player researches markets, reviews customer needs, hires employees, studies company performance, and purchases equipment or materials online. The garage serves as the first shop floor where production takes place. Initially, the player performs every role personally. They investigate market opportunities, design products, purchase materials, operate machines, package finished goods, and deliver products for shipment. This early stage is intended to create a direct connection between the player and every aspect of the business while establishing an understanding of how the company's systems interact.

The primary gameplay loop begins with market research. The market continuously generates needs representing the desires of potential customers. A need is fundamentally a collection of desired attributes and target values. For example, a market may desire products that are lightweight, highly durable, corrosion resistant, inexpensive, or precise. The player reviews these needs through the market interface and designs a sample product intended to meet those requirements. Once completed, the sample is submitted for evaluation. If the sample successfully satisfies the need, a customer order is generated. If the sample fails to demonstrate sufficient value, the player may revise the design or pursue a different opportunity. This process creates a clear relationship between market demand and manufacturing activity, ensuring that production is always driven by customer requirements rather than random experimentation.

Once an order has been secured, the manufacturing process begins. Materials are purchased from suppliers and delivered through the shipping system. Each material possesses a unique collection of attributes that define its behavior and value. Materials themselves are not products. Instead, they are the starting point from which parts are created. A part is defined by two things: its starting material and the complete ordered list of operations performed upon it. A mild steel component that has been drilled, tapped, and painted is fundamentally different from a mild steel component that has only been drilled. The operation history becomes part of the part's identity. This approach creates a highly scalable product definition system while avoiding the need for thousands of predefined items.

Manufacturing equipment exists to perform operations on materials and parts. Equipment includes machines such as drill presses, CNC mills, brake presses, saws, welders, and finishing systems. Machines do not create value by themselves. Their value comes from their ability to execute operations that transform attributes. Every operation alters some aspect of a part, either positively or negatively. Drilling may improve functionality while slightly increasing manufacturing cost. Powder coating may improve corrosion resistance while increasing processing time. Bending may improve structural characteristics while reducing flexibility. Through these interactions, complex products emerge from simple systems. Rather than hardcoding products individually, Startup Simulator allows products to evolve naturally from combinations of materials and operations.

Attributes form the universal language of the simulation. They are the common thread connecting every major system in the game. Materials possess attributes, operations modify attributes, needs request attributes, and customers purchase products because of attributes. This creates a single consistent mechanism through which all manufacturing decisions can be evaluated. The player is therefore not simply producing parts. The player is producing attribute values that satisfy market demand. By centering the simulation around attributes, the game gains flexibility while maintaining a coherent internal logic.

Individual parts may be sold directly, but they may also be combined into assemblies. An assembly is nothing more than a collection of parts grouped together into a single object. To simplify logistics and future system development, every standalone part is treated as an assembly containing exactly one part. This means that employees, shipping systems, storage systems, and production systems only need to understand assemblies rather than maintaining separate rule sets for parts and larger products. As products become more complex, assemblies simply grow to contain additional components. The underlying logistics system remains unchanged.

Production activities occur on the shop floor. The shop floor is the physical area where manufacturing equipment, materials, parts, and employees interact. Startup Simulator places significant emphasis on physical presence and movement. The player cannot instantly access menus from anywhere in the world. If the player wishes to research markets, they must travel to a computer. If the player wants to operate a machine, they must physically approach that machine. If the player intends to package and ship a product, they must move the product to the shipping area. This design philosophy exists to make facilities meaningful and to establish a strong connection between the player and the physical space of the company.

As orders grow larger, employees become necessary. Employees begin as simple manufacturing workers focused on logistics and operation execution. Their role is intentionally narrow during the early stages of development. Employees receive work instructions, move parts between locations, operate assigned equipment, and transfer completed work to the next stage of production. More advanced concepts such as skills, training, morale, certifications, leadership, and workplace disruptions are future expansion opportunities rather than initial requirements. The Employee framework must be designed to support these additions later while remaining simple and understandable in the first implementation.

Work instructions serve as the foundation of employee behavior. A work instruction does not represent a specific job or contract. Instead, it defines a repeatable manufacturing process for a particular type of part. The instruction tells a worker what operations remain to be completed, where the part should be obtained, which equipment should be used, and where the part should be delivered afterward. Because work instructions focus on missing operations rather than rigid sequences, the system naturally supports partially completed parts and process recovery without requiring complicated special-case logic. This flexibility will become increasingly valuable as production scales.

All manufactured products eventually flow through the shipping system. The shipping area represents the physical location where goods enter and leave the company. Early in the game, this area may consist of little more than a mailbox, driveway, or curbside pickup location. As the company expands into warehouses and industrial facilities, the shipping area grows into loading docks, staging zones, and truck terminals. Materials, equipment, and customer orders all move through this system. Deliveries require time, costs are incurred, and customers only provide payment when contractual requirements have been fulfilled. This ensures that logistics remain a meaningful component of operations throughout the game.

Underlying every system is the Master Clock. The Master Clock is arguably the most important system in Startup Simulator because every other mechanic depends upon it. Equipment cycle times, employee schedules, shipping arrivals, shipping departures, contract deadlines, market updates, payroll processing, lunch breaks, facility expenses, and customer demand generation all reference the same source of time. No system should maintain an independent timeline. Every object in the game world should defer to the Master Clock when determining whether an action can begin, whether an operation has finished, or whether an event should occur. This approach creates consistency throughout the simulation and prevents synchronization problems as the project grows.

The overall progression of Startup Simulator is therefore straightforward. The player researches needs, develops products, wins contracts, manufactures parts, ships orders, receives payment, and reinvests profits into growth. Increased profits lead to larger facilities, more advanced equipment, additional employees, and more sophisticated production capabilities. The player eventually transitions from a garage entrepreneur who performs every task personally into the owner of a thriving industrial operation supported by workers and automated processes. When cumulative profit reaches one million dollars, the player's business has achieved the status of a successful company and the game is considered complete. Importantly, completion is not the end. Just as in games such as Stardew Valley, post-victory play remains available for continued expansion, optimization, experimentation, and the pursuit of personal goals and achievements.

This framework establishes the intended direction of Startup Simulator. Markets generate demand. Demand creates orders. Orders drive manufacturing. Manufacturing transforms materials through equipment and labor. Completed products generate profit. Profit enables growth. Growth unlocks greater capabilities, creating a cycle that continually expands the scale and complexity of the player's company while remaining grounded in a simple and consistent set of systems. Every future feature should support this progression rather than distract from it, ensuring that the game's identity remains clear as development continues.
